# After Big Update: Check, Then Commit + Push

After **any big update** (large feature work, multi-file drift fixes, major refactors, multi-module changes — not tiny one-liners), do not treat the work as done until both steps below are complete.

## 1. Full check (required before done)

Run a full verification pass covering:

- App/bot/dashboard loads correctly
- Data/settings pull correctly
- Behavior is stable
- Loads quickly (no obvious regressions)
- No build errors or warnings (frontend build/tsc/lint as applicable; backend/tests as applicable)
- Commands work correctly (slash/relevant flows)
- Modules activate/enable correctly
- Full regression-style verification of what was touched, plus smoke of critical paths

Fix any failures from this check before proceeding.

## 2. Commit + push (default end state)

After the full check passes, **always commit and push** to GitHub.

Follow repo git safety:

- No force push
- No git config changes
- No secrets (`.env`, credentials, etc.)
- Commit message via HEREDOC
- Push to `origin` on the current branch

This **overrides** any earlier “don’t commit unless asked” preference for big updates. For big updates, check → commit → push is the default end state.

## Examples

```text
✅ BIG UPDATE — run full check, then commit + push
- New dashboard module across frontend + backend + bot
- Multi-file drift/i18n sync across locales
- Major lockdown/antiraid refactor touching core + routes + UI

❌ NOT a big update — do not auto commit/push
- One-line typo fix
- Single-file comment or copy tweak
- Tiny lint-only change
```
